# filipino-common-adjectives.kb - Filipino / Common adjectives
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 8 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
mahal | definition | Mahal means expensive or dear, and it is also how you say you love someone, depending on who you are talking to. The same word does both jobs, which is why context decides. A person is mahal, a price is mahal, and someone dear to you is also mahal. | expensive, dear, love, costly, price | what does mahal mean, is mahal expensive or love | mahal | mahal
mura | definition | Mura means cheap, and in everyday use it is nearly always about price rather than quality. Saying a thing is mura is not the same as saying it is bad, though sellers sometimes use it that way. Careful with the opposite, because mura can also mean gentle or soft in other contexts. | cheap, inexpensive, low price, affordable | what does mura mean, how do you say cheap in tagalog | mura | mura
kulang | definition | Kulang means lacking or insufficient, and it is the word you reach for when something is not enough. It applies to a budget that will not cover the bills, to sleep that was too short, and to a skill you have not built yet. Its opposite is sobra, which means excess rather than sufficiency, so saying hindi kulang is not quite the same as saying sobra. | lacking, insufficient, not enough, short | what does kulang mean, what is the opposite of kulang | kulang | kulang
sobra | definition | Sobra means excess or too much, and it describes having more than is needed rather than having a lot. Too much sugar is sobra ang asin, and someone who is being unreasonable is sobra na. The paired word is kulang for not enough, and Filipino treats these as the two ends of the same scale. | excess, too much, extra, too | what does sobra mean, difference between sobra and kulang | sobra | sobra
maganda | definition | Maganda means good, nice or beautiful, and it covers all three because Filipino does not separate them the way English does. It describes weather, food, a person and an idea without changing form. The opposite is hindi maganda or masama for something bad, and note that masama is stronger, closer to evil or serious. | good, nice, beautiful, quality | what does maganda mean, how do you say good in tagalog | maganda | maayo
masama | definition | Masama means bad or wrong, and it is a strong word that leans towards evil or serious rather than a mild negative. A small mistake is hindi maganda rather than masama. It is also the word used for a person behaving badly, so describing someone as masama carries real judgement rather than a description. | bad, evil, wrong, serious | what does masama mean, how do you say bad in tagalog | masama | sayop
malaki | definition | Malaki means big or large, and it is used for physical size, for quantities and for importance. A big favour is malaki ang tulong, and a large city is malaki ang lungsod. The opposite is maliit, and the same word also means a lot, so malaking pabor is both a big and a great favour. | big, large, malaki, size, plenty | what does malaki mean, how do you say big in tagalog | malaki | dako
maliit | definition | Maliit means small, and it is the everyday word for physical size and for quantity. It is the direct opposite of malaki, and unlike some size words in English it has no second meaning, so it cannot be misread in a sentence. Maliit na lang means only small, which is often a way of minimising something. | small, little, maliit, size, minor | what does maliit mean, how do you say small in tagalog | maliit | gamay
